WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.089 --> 00:00:05.130
Season 1 of House of the Dragon is almost
as good and if not just as good as the first

00:00:05.130 --> 00:00:07.049
four seasons of game of thrones.

00:00:07.049 --> 00:00:11.031
That’s a very high commendation to pin onto
the show, but I think it’s more leaning

00:00:11.031 --> 00:00:15.030
towards the almost as good since there were
a couple of aspects holding the season back

00:00:15.030 --> 00:00:16.480
from perfection.

00:00:16.480 --> 00:00:20.880
Firstly let’s go into the glaring positives
and talk about the plot of the season.

00:00:20.880 --> 00:00:24.880
House of the Dragon is in a weird spot where
the meat of the story is later on and right

00:00:24.880 --> 00:00:27.939
now they primarily have to do a tonne of set-up.

00:00:27.939 --> 00:00:32.380
Most shows given the same task would be pretty
terrible since they’d be overly expository

00:00:32.380 --> 00:00:36.430
driven and the plot wouldn’t be engaging
enough to enthrall the audience.

00:00:36.430 --> 00:00:41.230
But here in House of the Dragon, they somehow
make the story riveting throughout every episode,

00:00:41.230 --> 00:00:46.399
despite the limitations of the story not being
as grand or crazy as Game of Thrones.

00:00:46.399 --> 00:00:51.040
They do this by having a consistent presence
of entertaining conflict that simultaneously

00:00:51.040 --> 00:00:54.309
explores the intricacies of every character.

00:00:54.309 --> 00:00:58.059
We don’t just sit here and have someone
tell us the histories of house Targaryen or

00:00:58.059 --> 00:01:02.769
house Hightower, and instead, we witness the
rich characters work to overcome heaps of

00:01:02.769 --> 00:01:07.530
obstacles that simultaneously builds them
into concrete characters with fully established

00:01:07.530 --> 00:01:09.540
traits and personalities.

00:01:09.540 --> 00:01:13.932
Even side characters like Rhaenys and Corlys
are incredibly detailed and scenes in which

00:01:13.932 --> 00:01:18.471
we explore their complex motivations like
Corlys’ quest for power and Rhaenys’ love

00:01:18.471 --> 00:01:21.070
for her family are entrancing.

00:01:21.070 --> 00:01:25.810
This is essentially catalyzed by the debacle
of wanting to get entangled in royal politics,

00:01:25.810 --> 00:01:30.640
so the conflict works for both interesting
story progression and character progression.

00:01:30.640 --> 00:01:34.710
Each episode has a micro problem that needs
to be solved and that’s something a lot

00:01:34.710 --> 00:01:36.670
of shows forget.

00:01:36.670 --> 00:01:40.720
Normally most time and attention is spent
on the macro problems, but here, the emphasis

00:01:40.720 --> 00:01:43.990
is put on small conflicts that develop characters.

00:01:43.990 --> 00:01:48.820
For other shows, these minor obstacles generally
act as filler, however that isn’t the case

00:01:48.820 --> 00:01:49.820
here.

00:01:49.820 --> 00:01:54.750
Whether that’s Rhaenyra overcoming the responsibilities
of marriage, childbirth, loss, or love, the

00:01:54.750 --> 00:01:59.420
writers essentially send every character down
an insane obstacle course of conflict that

00:01:59.420 --> 00:02:01.500
sets them up for the oncoming war.

00:02:01.500 --> 00:02:05.460
That on its own is pretty impressive from
a writing standpoint, but what makes this

00:02:05.460 --> 00:02:10.750
even crazier to grasp, is having so many scenes
with an ensemble of main characters interacting

00:02:10.750 --> 00:02:11.920
with one another.

00:02:11.920 --> 00:02:15.670
This is something that David and Dan really
dropped the ball on for how underdeveloped

00:02:15.670 --> 00:02:20.110
and awkward their group scenes were in the
later seasons, but the writers in this show

00:02:20.110 --> 00:02:21.890
handle it very well.

00:02:21.890 --> 00:02:26.330
Whether that’s the last supper, the funeral
of Laena, the wedding dinner, or any small

00:02:26.330 --> 00:02:27.450
council scene.

00:02:27.450 --> 00:02:31.590
The attention to detail and thought that’s
put into every microscopic character beat

00:02:31.590 --> 00:02:34.560
floors me and the character gets to shine
brightly.

00:02:34.560 --> 00:02:39.450
The coverage always accounts for small character
reactions that act as development for them

00:02:39.450 --> 00:02:41.310
that doesn’t require dialogue.

00:02:41.310 --> 00:02:45.000
Like the entire post-funeral scene where every
character is glaring at one another and just

00:02:45.000 --> 00:02:48.580
from their expressions alone, you can easily
tell their views of one another.

00:02:48.580 --> 00:02:52.860
The ensemble scenes are always a joy to watch
and it never feels like the writers are leaving

00:02:52.860 --> 00:02:54.629
out any important interactions.

00:02:54.629 --> 00:02:59.280
Like when you think about it in Game of Thrones
season 8, why did Arya never have a scene

00:02:59.280 --> 00:03:04.340
with Daenerys given that she idolized the
independent and badass nature of her ancestors,

00:03:04.340 --> 00:03:08.560
or even character pairings that last less
than a minute and needed much more time before

00:03:08.560 --> 00:03:09.920
cutting away.

00:03:09.920 --> 00:03:13.670
House of the Dragon never feels like they
are missing out on development and every character

00:03:13.670 --> 00:03:16.280
interaction and moment is fully realized.

00:03:16.280 --> 00:03:20.110
We aren’t getting thrown out of scenes jarringly
like Jon Snow being on the verge of telling

00:03:20.110 --> 00:03:24.390
his sisters his identity, and instead, we
have a complete beginning, middle, and end

00:03:24.390 --> 00:03:25.390
for each scene.

00:03:25.390 --> 00:03:30.280
None of that bullshit lazy writing from season
8 is here and it’s refreshing that the writers

00:03:30.280 --> 00:03:34.420
are competent at writing long and fully fleshed-out
dialogue scenes.

00:03:34.420 --> 00:03:37.560
Which on the subject of dialogue, it’s pretty
good here.

00:03:37.560 --> 00:03:42.340
Now it isn’t as outstanding as it is in
Game of Thrones given that it’s pulled directly

00:03:42.340 --> 00:03:43.360
from George RR.

00:03:43.360 --> 00:03:46.860
Martin, but I think the writers still did
a great job in house of the dragon.

00:03:46.860 --> 00:03:50.700
I’m honestly surprised and did not have
high hopes for the dialogue going into the

00:03:50.700 --> 00:03:54.230
show since the source material doesn’t really
have any to draw from.

00:03:54.230 --> 00:03:58.470
And they still managed to make some quotable
lines that still stick with me after finishing

00:03:58.470 --> 00:03:59.600
the season.

00:03:59.600 --> 00:04:03.810
I will not sit here and suffer crows that
have come to feast on their corpses.

00:04:03.810 --> 00:04:07.319
Not to be suffocated by all this f***ing politicking.

00:04:07.319 --> 00:04:14.360
What is this brief mortal life, if not the
pursuit of legacy?

00:04:14.360 --> 00:04:16.930
History doesn’t remember blood, it remembers
names.

00:04:16.930 --> 00:04:20.260
She can have her answer now stuffed in her
father’s mouth along with his withered cock.

00:04:20.260 --> 00:04:23.320
Let’s end this mummers farce.

00:04:23.320 --> 00:04:27.229
So even though the dialogue isn’t technically
as good as Game of Thrones, that doesn’t

00:04:27.229 --> 00:04:28.950
mean it’s not great.

00:04:28.950 --> 00:04:33.070
Because Game of Thrones-level dialogue is
a very hard bar to clear on its own.

00:04:33.070 --> 00:04:36.540
Overall for the story, it was still able to
be really entertaining without relying on

00:04:36.540 --> 00:04:40.220
any major twists to throw the narrative into
the stratosphere.

00:04:40.220 --> 00:04:44.880
Especially since each episode had some form
of major confrontation going on that always

00:04:44.880 --> 00:04:49.060
kept the audience engaged with what was happening
with every character.

00:04:49.060 --> 00:04:53.130
Because of this, no episode felt underwhelming
and the show felt really consistent in terms

00:04:53.130 --> 00:04:55.620
of the strength of the plot.

00:04:55.620 --> 00:04:59.060
Looking at the characters, I think we have
an amazing roster here.

00:04:59.060 --> 00:05:02.479
To me, the standout is definitely Viserys
Targaryen.

00:05:02.479 --> 00:05:06.190
The way his character balances wanting to
keep things peaceful while not wanting to

00:05:06.190 --> 00:05:10.560
overly address conflict is an endearingly
wholesome quality despite it not being a very

00:05:10.560 --> 00:05:12.360
good trait for a king.

00:05:12.360 --> 00:05:16.630
Since he’s such a pure-hearted character
it’s a tragedy that he meets such an agonizing

00:05:16.630 --> 00:05:18.050
and slow death.

00:05:18.050 --> 00:05:22.350
This transformation is so jaw-dropping and
the ability to visually track his decay throughout

00:05:22.350 --> 00:05:25.250
the season was a great idea from the writers.

00:05:25.250 --> 00:05:29.280
Paddy Considine seamlessly blends between
these points of taking on increasingly more

00:05:29.280 --> 00:05:33.090
pain and taking on the burden of trying to
hide it and appear stronger.

00:05:33.090 --> 00:05:37.350
I love the small details starting out where
you can subtly see he’s hurting on the inside

00:05:37.350 --> 00:05:39.199
but he doesn’t want to show it.

00:05:39.199 --> 00:05:43.490
That meticulously planned-out acting of focusing
on the intricacies is brilliant and seeing

00:05:43.490 --> 00:05:47.910
it progress towards the later and more grievous
stages of his death was outstanding.

00:05:47.910 --> 00:05:52.080
What I think is single-handedly the best moment
in the show is Viserys smiling at the last

00:05:52.080 --> 00:05:55.730
supper and witnessing his family be happy
for the last time.

00:05:55.730 --> 00:06:00.110
It makes me cry thinking that he died a happy
man and this was the last drop of pure joy

00:06:00.110 --> 00:06:03.130
he could witness in his sea of misery.

00:06:03.130 --> 00:06:07.530
Every scene with Viserys was great and I’m
rooting for Paddy Considine to get an Emmy

00:06:07.530 --> 00:06:08.530
win.

00:06:08.530 --> 00:06:10.330
The next standout character is definitely
Daemon.

00:06:10.330 --> 00:06:12.949
It’s kind of hard to describe the man.

00:06:12.949 --> 00:06:19.560
He’s cunning, smart, suave, sexy, unpredictable,
brave, and loves to get his hands dirty.

00:06:19.560 --> 00:06:23.949
He’s kind of the likeable wildcard of the
group and because of that, it makes him so

00:06:23.949 --> 00:06:24.949
intriguing.

00:06:24.949 --> 00:06:28.530
Not to mention that Matt Smith is pretty goated
and it feels like he slips right into the

00:06:28.530 --> 00:06:29.530
character.

00:06:29.530 --> 00:06:33.050
Otto Hightower is just an absolute babe and
you can’t dispute that.

00:06:33.050 --> 00:06:39.440
Rhys Ifan and his hypnotizing gravely voice
made every line he spoke so varied and detailed.

00:06:39.440 --> 00:06:43.479
I played a montage of him saying your grace
in a prior review so I feel like I should

00:06:43.479 --> 00:06:44.680
play that again here.

00:06:44.680 --> 00:06:47.150
Well must I say it your grace?

00:06:47.150 --> 00:06:48.150
Your graceheh.

00:06:48.150 --> 00:06:49.150
Your grace.

00:06:49.150 --> 00:06:50.150
Your Grace.

00:06:50.150 --> 00:06:51.250
I’m sorry your grace.

00:06:51.250 --> 00:06:53.669
That’s hardly how I viewed it your grace
eh.

00:06:53.669 --> 00:06:55.800
Well you honor me your grace.

00:06:55.800 --> 00:06:57.000
Yourghh graceee.

00:06:57.000 --> 00:07:01.389
The character in essence embodies what I love
so much about this universe which is the political

00:07:01.389 --> 00:07:04.389
manipulation that he channels really well.

00:07:04.389 --> 00:07:08.379
Episode 9 where he’s ruling with an iron
fist and making people change allegiances

00:07:08.379 --> 00:07:12.230
was extremely chilling and I can’t wait
to see more of that in the next season.

00:07:12.230 --> 00:07:16.880
For Rhaenyra, I’d say I liked the younger
actress a bit more, and not saying that Emma

00:07:16.880 --> 00:07:20.750
D’arcy was bad by any means and she did
a great job in showing a more mature version

00:07:20.750 --> 00:07:25.879
of Rhaenyra, but the emotional depths that
Milly goes to for young Rhaenyra was astounding.

00:07:25.879 --> 00:07:29.480
Any scene in which she’s exploring the hardships
of growing up and dealing with that immense

00:07:29.480 --> 00:07:31.900
conflict of responsibility was engrossing.

00:07:31.900 --> 00:07:37.090
I love how she’ll go through a journey in
each episode and then arrives at a major confrontation

00:07:37.090 --> 00:07:39.790
that severely changes her as a character.

00:07:39.790 --> 00:07:43.110
And that’s normally shared between her and
Viserys and their scenes together were so

00:07:43.110 --> 00:07:44.479
precious.

00:07:44.479 --> 00:07:47.849
Such a good actor and I’m excited to see
her career take off from here.

00:07:47.849 --> 00:07:51.289
For Allicent, I liked both actresses pretty
equally.

00:07:51.289 --> 00:07:55.130
I think Emily Carey was a bit more reserved
in terms of breaking and getting upset and

00:07:55.130 --> 00:08:00.879
holding everything in, which she did perfectly,
meanwhile, Olivia on the other hand, was really

00:08:00.879 --> 00:08:03.419
good at unleashing those emotions and exploding.

00:08:03.419 --> 00:08:07.920
The scene within episode 7 where she rushes
Rhaenyra with a blade was an amazing showcase

00:08:07.920 --> 00:08:11.879
for her and she’s just so goddamn expressive
with her eyes.

00:08:11.879 --> 00:08:15.980
Then I really like how they made Allicent
have a strong moral conscience in comparison

00:08:15.980 --> 00:08:16.980
to Otto.

00:08:16.980 --> 00:08:20.159
It kind of gives you someone to root for on
the green side of the coin and allows you

00:08:20.159 --> 00:08:21.730
to sympathize with them.

00:08:21.730 --> 00:08:26.000
Some people may prefer the more bold and evil
villains like Joffrey and Ramsay, but having

00:08:26.000 --> 00:08:30.039
a villain who isn’t entirely bad and is
more self-serving is refreshing.

00:08:30.039 --> 00:08:34.850
That’s why I think Tywin is the best villain
in Game of Thrones because he's a good guy

00:08:34.850 --> 00:08:38.260
at the center of his story, but he does terrible
things.

00:08:38.260 --> 00:08:41.130
And the same can be said for the hightowers.

00:08:41.130 --> 00:08:45.190
Technically what they are doing is wrong since
they are defying Viserys’ wishes, but it’s

00:08:45.190 --> 00:08:47.990
realistic to try to seize the power for themselves.

00:08:47.990 --> 00:08:52.589
Along with the fact that having a male ruler
is probably more supported by the common people.

00:08:52.589 --> 00:08:56.800
So I really enjoy this civil war style conflict
in which you have characters on either side

00:08:56.800 --> 00:08:58.250
that you can agree with.

00:08:58.250 --> 00:09:01.280
Although the blacks are much more portrayed
as the good guys here.

00:09:01.280 --> 00:09:05.140
We’ll see if that remains to be the case
since Rhaenyra will probably do some heinous

00:09:05.140 --> 00:09:09.269
things next season from how mad she was in
the ending scene.

00:09:09.269 --> 00:09:13.870
One major aspect of House of the Dragon that
is a significant step up above Game of Thrones,

00:09:13.870 --> 00:09:16.020
is the increased production value.

00:09:16.020 --> 00:09:22.620
The cinematography, music, set pieces, locations,
and VFX were all expertly done and since they

00:09:22.620 --> 00:09:27.470
had such a high budget to pump up these production
pillars, the show was able to flourish.

00:09:27.470 --> 00:09:31.589
The cinematography in particular was very
different in comparison to Game of Thrones,

00:09:31.589 --> 00:09:34.730
and so much to the point that it was kind
of jarring at first.

00:09:34.730 --> 00:09:38.920
Because the first few seasons of GoT feel
more grounded and naturalistic in their approach,

00:09:38.920 --> 00:09:42.709
while here, the lighting in particular feels
more cinematic.

00:09:42.709 --> 00:09:47.209
So many scenes had stark contrasts that made
them feel moodier and more dramatic.

00:09:47.209 --> 00:09:49.990
Along with their overall color grade playing
into that feeling.

00:09:49.990 --> 00:09:54.899
Also, I think there were more impressive shots
like anything within the stepstones was delicious,

00:09:54.899 --> 00:09:59.880
how they accentuated key character moments,
more usage of shallow focus to bring out depth,

00:09:59.880 --> 00:10:04.070
and more dynamic shots like rack focusing
between two different characters.

00:10:04.070 --> 00:10:08.700
Every scene is uplifted by this great cinematography
and makes it feel like you are actually watching

00:10:08.700 --> 00:10:10.550
something that’s expensive.

00:10:10.550 --> 00:10:15.220
For locations, initially, I thought they were
going to overly rely on the volume to film

00:10:15.220 --> 00:10:18.220
scenes, but they actually went to some beautiful
places.

00:10:18.220 --> 00:10:21.779
For example, locations for Dragonstone were
basically filmed on a mountain.

00:10:21.779 --> 00:10:26.100
It was so hard to film there that they had
to helicopter in equipment and set up tonnes

00:10:26.100 --> 00:10:27.100
of infrastructure.

00:10:27.100 --> 00:10:31.470
The result from all this work pays off because
it fleshes out the world when you can realistically

00:10:31.470 --> 00:10:34.850
film in it, instead of faking it in the volume.

00:10:34.850 --> 00:10:39.980
Not to say that the volume is bad, but overly
relying on it hurts the scale of your show.

00:10:39.980 --> 00:10:45.161
However, when they do use it, like the confrontation
scenes outside of Dragonstone, it’s seamless

00:10:45.161 --> 00:10:48.529
because it fits into the already large collection
of real locations.

00:10:48.529 --> 00:10:52.420
That’s a great way to make these fictional
places feel real.

00:10:52.420 --> 00:10:56.339
Since the creators had a bigger budget, it
just feels so liberating for them to fully

00:10:56.339 --> 00:10:59.300
spend it and not hold back on where they wanted
to film.

00:10:59.300 --> 00:11:03.380
Like these large beaches during low tide in
which the crew only had a small window of

00:11:03.380 --> 00:11:06.850
time to film was probably pretty expensive
to shoot at.

00:11:06.850 --> 00:11:11.110
But it pays off because the footage they manage
to capture was gorgeous.

00:11:11.110 --> 00:11:15.139
Then for the sets, they did not hold back
at all and they basically made the entirety

00:11:15.139 --> 00:11:17.950
of the fictional locations they sought to
create.

00:11:17.950 --> 00:11:22.720
The red keep is just one big set with a bunch
of connecting rooms and Dragonstone is grander

00:11:22.720 --> 00:11:24.960
on top of having a much better atmosphere.

00:11:24.960 --> 00:11:30.180
The steam permeating from the ground and even
the war table glowing up were such nice touches

00:11:30.180 --> 00:11:32.090
that weren’t in Game of Thrones.

00:11:32.090 --> 00:11:36.290
It’s also more logical to spare no expense
on these sets because of how many scenes take

00:11:36.290 --> 00:11:37.290
place here.

00:11:37.290 --> 00:11:41.050
It allows the filmmakers to have a lot more
versatility within these scenes when they

00:11:41.050 --> 00:11:43.530
are able to shoot in any direction.

00:11:43.530 --> 00:11:47.470
Like my favorite showcase was during the oner
of Rhaenyra walking with her baby to Queen

00:11:47.470 --> 00:11:52.170
Allicent and they essentially walk through
the entire set, showing off its pure scale.

00:11:52.170 --> 00:11:57.510
I truly wasn’t disappointed by any setpiece
in the show and even the VFX was outstanding.

00:11:57.510 --> 00:12:02.370
It’s nice that HBO has had a lot of practice
doing dragons because they look fantastic

00:12:02.370 --> 00:12:03.730
in this show.

00:12:03.730 --> 00:12:07.839
Primarily due to the fact that they have different
and distinct designs and don’t look like

00:12:07.839 --> 00:12:10.110
carbon copies of one another anymore.

00:12:10.110 --> 00:12:14.910
This also allows each dragon to show off a
certain personality like the scary and unsettling

00:12:14.910 --> 00:12:17.300
way Caraxes noodles around his neck.

00:12:17.300 --> 00:12:21.760
Or granny Vaghar slowly moving around given
how massive she is.

00:12:21.760 --> 00:12:25.660
Which they also did a great job in communicating
the scale of the dragons.

00:12:25.660 --> 00:12:29.620
Like Aemond having to climb up Vaghar like
she was a tower or whenever they position

00:12:29.620 --> 00:12:33.260
a dragon near one of our main characters as
a size comparison.

00:12:33.260 --> 00:12:37.310
They do not shy away from showing off their
dragons and that’s also a refreshing aspect

00:12:37.310 --> 00:12:40.649
because you can fully see them in any night
scene.

00:12:40.649 --> 00:12:45.670
Generally, when VFX is done at night it’s
always intentionally done to hide the VFX,

00:12:45.670 --> 00:12:49.920
but during Vaghar’s two flight scenes, you
can clearly see what’s going on.

00:12:49.920 --> 00:12:53.940
And since they don’t hide anything it makes
for some insanely good scenes.

00:12:53.940 --> 00:12:57.680
Vaghar’s last scene in the finale because
of this, was easily one of the best from how

00:12:57.680 --> 00:13:00.490
terrifying and scary it was.

00:13:00.490 --> 00:13:02.120
Truly a very immersive experience.

00:13:02.120 --> 00:13:07.630
Then that’s just the visual side of things
because Ramin Djwadi is back for the score.

00:13:07.630 --> 00:13:11.779
The man is still killing it and the new themes
he created for the Velaryons and Rhaenyra

00:13:11.779 --> 00:13:13.130
were so good.

00:13:13.130 --> 00:13:17.300
The chorus chants of Rahenyra’s theme immediately
gives you this uplifted feeling that is so

00:13:17.300 --> 00:13:19.010
addictive to listen to.

00:13:19.010 --> 00:13:23.360
I also love how it all culminates in her ending
scene when she becomes queen in the track

00:13:23.360 --> 00:13:27.150
called “The Crown of Jaehaerys”, I’d
definitely recommend checking it out along

00:13:27.150 --> 00:13:29.621
with “The Power of Prophecy”.

00:13:29.621 --> 00:13:33.870
Then the Velaryon theme sounds so unique with
its tribal-like woodwinds playing with drums

00:13:33.870 --> 00:13:35.510
and violins accompanying it.

00:13:35.510 --> 00:13:40.389
It’s so fresh and distinct that it makes
me crave to see Ramin Djawadi flex and experiment

00:13:40.389 --> 00:13:42.390
more within Westeros.

00:13:42.390 --> 00:13:46.170
And since we are on the subject of themes,
it’s kind of disappointing that there wasn’t

00:13:46.170 --> 00:13:50.750
a new main theme for House of the Dragon,
and they instead used the one from Game of

00:13:50.750 --> 00:13:51.750
Thrones.

00:13:51.750 --> 00:13:55.149
It would make the most sense to me to combine
the themes of Rhaenyra and house Targaryen

00:13:55.149 --> 00:13:57.399
for the title sequence but oh well.

00:13:57.399 --> 00:14:01.570
I think this sets more of a lazy precedence
for other spin-off shows that are in development

00:14:01.570 --> 00:14:03.570
and hope they don’t follow down the same
path.

00:14:03.570 --> 00:14:08.240
Because it would be weird to hear the same
game of thrones theme in every single show.

00:14:08.240 --> 00:14:13.370
Especially since the upcoming Sea Snake show
as an example, already has such a cool theme.

00:14:13.370 --> 00:14:17.860
But yeah, since the budget for House of the
dragon is so big, the production value of

00:14:17.860 --> 00:14:22.180
this season is able to ascend and the result
is truly amazing.

00:14:22.180 --> 00:14:26.980
So after going through all of these scintillating
positives, let's talk about some of the negatives

00:14:26.980 --> 00:14:32.050
that bring the show down a little bit; starting
with the pace at which the story moves.

00:14:32.050 --> 00:14:35.800
Not in the sense of how quickly each scene
plays out because if anything the writers

00:14:35.800 --> 00:14:40.380
take their time on a scene-to-scene basis,
but more of the fact of how fast time moves

00:14:40.380 --> 00:14:42.010
between episodes.

00:14:42.010 --> 00:14:43.740
AKA time jumps.

00:14:43.740 --> 00:14:47.510
Now it’s really hard to avoid this fact
given that the writers have to cover about

00:14:47.510 --> 00:14:51.010
20 to 30 years of time within 10 episodes.

00:14:51.010 --> 00:14:55.180
Personally, I think it would have made more
sense to do a longer season so they didn’t

00:14:55.180 --> 00:15:00.060
have to skip so much time and avoid that jarring
feeling of having to restart the show again.

00:15:00.060 --> 00:15:03.910
I think Lancel needs to get the showrunners
the season stretcher.

00:15:03.910 --> 00:15:07.850
Because the main issue with doing these time
jumps is firstly, you are skipping over a

00:15:07.850 --> 00:15:09.600
lot of plotlines.

00:15:09.600 --> 00:15:13.980
My biggest gripe is getting thrown into these
developments at the mid/endpoint and we didn’t

00:15:13.980 --> 00:15:15.579
get any buildup for them.

00:15:15.579 --> 00:15:20.389
The worst examples of this is Ser Harwin Strong’s
relationship with Rhaenyra and Daemon’s

00:15:20.389 --> 00:15:22.620
relationship with Laena Velaryon.

00:15:22.620 --> 00:15:27.440
Within episode 6, both Ser Harwin and Laena
die and you don’t feel that sad for them

00:15:27.440 --> 00:15:31.310
because there was no time to build an emotional
attachment to these characters.

00:15:31.310 --> 00:15:35.260
The whole point of build-up is to make us
care about them and understand who they are.

00:15:35.260 --> 00:15:39.350
So when you don’t include it and throw their
death at us suddenly, then we are going to

00:15:39.350 --> 00:15:42.620
feel detached and the death has a soft impact.

00:15:42.620 --> 00:15:46.370
That and it’s also just confusing to see
a character go from killing their wife to

00:15:46.370 --> 00:15:49.920
then being in a family with a bunch of kids
in the next episode.

00:15:49.920 --> 00:15:54.420
So having to essentially re-meet these characters
and have to be introduced to what is happening

00:15:54.420 --> 00:15:59.010
again shouldn’t be the aim of the writers,
and instead, naturally get to that point by

00:15:59.010 --> 00:16:01.540
taking more time to tell your story.

00:16:01.540 --> 00:16:04.600
Then there’s also the aspect of how old
the actors look.

00:16:04.600 --> 00:16:09.040
Now to me, I don’t really care about this
aspect that much, but it does become visually

00:16:09.040 --> 00:16:13.990
confusing when someone like Criston Cole doesn’t
age at all within the 20+ years the show takes

00:16:13.990 --> 00:16:17.950
place in comparison to other characters who
age a lot.

00:16:17.950 --> 00:16:22.670
Like hell, the character Aemond gets born
in-between episodes 5 and 6, and he looks

00:16:22.670 --> 00:16:27.069
just as old and if not older than Criston
Cole by episode 8.

00:16:27.069 --> 00:16:31.360
So to that degree, it feels a bit inconsistent
in terms of how they age up the characters,

00:16:31.360 --> 00:16:34.800
but you kind of have to just accept that aspect
of the show.

00:16:34.800 --> 00:16:40.470
Because the only option they have is to constantly
keep changing to older actors or use makeup,

00:16:40.470 --> 00:16:43.040
which using makeup I don’t think would really
work.

00:16:43.040 --> 00:16:47.709
So to change actors every 10 years within
the show would be even more jarring than just

00:16:47.709 --> 00:16:49.290
having the same one to me.

00:16:49.290 --> 00:16:53.029
The fans were already losing their minds when
they swapped out Milly and Emily for their

00:16:53.029 --> 00:16:54.250
older counterparts.

00:16:54.250 --> 00:16:58.040
So doing that more frequently I think would
be much more annoying.

00:16:58.040 --> 00:17:02.130
So I think this just comes down to people
having to accept that part of the show since

00:17:02.130 --> 00:17:04.780
there isn’t a better alternative to take.

00:17:04.780 --> 00:17:08.539
For the last flaw of the season, there are
a couple of big changes the writers make within

00:17:08.539 --> 00:17:12.910
the story that I think needed to be re-written
or needed some time to breathe.

00:17:12.910 --> 00:17:16.770
The first example of this is Criston Cole
snapping and killing Joffrey.

00:17:16.770 --> 00:17:20.880
The reason why this development doesn’t
work well is that they immediately do a massive

00:17:20.880 --> 00:17:23.049
time jump after it happens.

00:17:23.049 --> 00:17:28.230
So any big questions relating to this event
are instantly disregarded and aren’t addressed

00:17:28.230 --> 00:17:29.230
again.

00:17:29.230 --> 00:17:33.020
Like will there be any repercussions for Criston
Cole doing this, does Allicent defend him

00:17:33.020 --> 00:17:37.690
from being kicked out of the Kingsguard, does
Criston lie about the event, and even if he

00:17:37.690 --> 00:17:42.240
does wouldn’t that further taint his honor
that he cares about, and do any of the witnesses

00:17:42.240 --> 00:17:43.510
care to make a statement?

00:17:43.510 --> 00:17:47.890
It doesn’t make sense to hit the skip button
right when we get to the peak of Criston’s

00:17:47.890 --> 00:17:48.890
arc.

00:17:48.890 --> 00:17:53.240
There are so many aspects surrounding this
major story development that need to be resolved

00:17:53.240 --> 00:17:57.460
and the fact that the writers do a time jump
over all of it is insane to me.

00:17:57.460 --> 00:18:01.270
This kind of ties back to the repercussions
of doing time jumps but I also think this

00:18:01.270 --> 00:18:03.450
was a sloppily written scene.

00:18:03.450 --> 00:18:06.750
Because there are some contrivances within
this scene like the crowd randomly turning

00:18:06.750 --> 00:18:12.030
into a moshpit for no reason, other than to
forcibly instill a moment of confusion and

00:18:12.030 --> 00:18:13.030
chaos.

00:18:13.030 --> 00:18:17.020
Going into another big change they made that
I feel like needed to be reworked, we have

00:18:17.020 --> 00:18:21.200
Rhaenys bursting through the floor of Aegon’s
coronation and letting the greens live.

00:18:21.200 --> 00:18:25.480
In the book, she wasn’t even in Kingslanding
when this all went down and the writers made

00:18:25.480 --> 00:18:29.390
this change to quote on quote give Rhaenys
a bigger moment to shine.

00:18:29.390 --> 00:18:33.799
Which firstly, that doesn’t really track
well because Rhaenys here is killing hundreds

00:18:33.799 --> 00:18:35.280
of bystanders.

00:18:35.280 --> 00:18:39.630
So this immediately deteriorates my view of
her since she probably could have taken another

00:18:39.630 --> 00:18:40.840
exit within the caves.

00:18:40.840 --> 00:18:45.429
Then secondly, you are basically asking the
audience to get annoyed when a character has

00:18:45.429 --> 00:18:50.370
the option to eliminate the entire threat
of the show but chooses not to.

00:18:50.370 --> 00:18:54.450
Now I do agree with the character decision
here for Rhaenys, however, I feel like the

00:18:54.450 --> 00:18:59.179
writers should have made her choice ambiguous,
by establishing an external conflcit that

00:18:59.179 --> 00:19:01.380
forces Rhaneys out of the scene.

00:19:01.380 --> 00:19:05.570
Like another dragon showing up from the pit
or archers being posted up about to shoot

00:19:05.570 --> 00:19:06.570
her.

00:19:06.570 --> 00:19:10.451
With these misfires of endings, it hurts an
already good episode and makes them end with

00:19:10.451 --> 00:19:11.490
a whimper.

00:19:11.490 --> 00:19:15.780
In the grand scheme of things, this isn’t
a major seasonal issue, but it does impact

00:19:15.780 --> 00:19:16.890
two episodes.

00:19:16.890 --> 00:19:21.990
So yeah, going into House of the Dragon I
was not expecting the show to be this good.

00:19:21.990 --> 00:19:26.010
Given that Ryan Condal didn’t have an impressive
slate of past work and the source material

00:19:26.010 --> 00:19:28.790
offered little to no dialogue to work off
of.

00:19:28.790 --> 00:19:32.870
So the fact that they were able to make a
season this great really blows me away.

00:19:32.870 --> 00:19:36.760
I’m immensely looking forward to season
2 and since the story will most likely be

00:19:36.760 --> 00:19:41.470
more consistently riveting than this season
on a story basis, I’m very excited.

00:19:41.470 --> 00:19:45.470
Thank you for watching and if you are at all
curious as to what I think about other popular

00:19:45.470 --> 00:19:48.280
movies, then I’ve probably rated them on
letterboxd.

00:19:48.280 --> 00:19:52.120
I generally don’t have enough time to make
videos on everything I watch so if you’re

00:19:52.120 --> 00:19:55.080
curious you can follow me on there and see
what I’m watching currently.

00:19:55.080 --> 00:19:58.850
I haven’t really put any attention on this,
so yeah, the link will be in the description

00:19:58.850 --> 00:20:00.650
and thank you in advance if you follow me
on there.

